{
 "id": "2025-huang-visual",
 "name": "Visual Walkability of Commercial Streets (Fukuoka Tenjin)",
 "description": "Method replication: same task, scales and design as the paper; the images are not the paper's stimuli (new Google Street View panoramas of the same district, © Google). Huang, Yu, Lyu, Sun, Dewancker & Gao (2025, Buildings 15:113) measured the visual walkability of the Tenjin commercial district in Fukuoka, Japan. Observers looked at 360° Google Street View panoramas (2048 × 624, pitch 0°) and rated each on four walking needs with 5-point semantic differential scales: Useless (1)–Useful (5), Unsafe–Safe, Uncomfortable–Comfortable, Unattractive–Attractive, after reading a definition of each need. Mean scores over 475 panoramas were attractiveness 3.25 > safety 3.02 > comfort 2.97 > usefulness 2.93; regressions on semantic-segmentation features found walkable space and landmark visibility drove usefulness, cleanliness drove safety, greenness drove comfort and landmark visibility drove attractiveness. Analysis as in the paper: mean and SD per need and per image, correlations between the four needs, then image-level scores joined with segmentation features (greenness, sky, walkable space, enclosure, cleanliness) for correlation and stepwise linear regression. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper rated all 475 panoramas sampled every 50 m along OSM roads (April 2023 captures); this template bundles 45 panoramas (2022–2026) from random points in the same district, and each participant rates 20 of them in random order; (2) the paper projected slides in a classroom for 40 s per image with 35 student observers; here participants view on their own screen with no time limit (the page asks for 30–40 s); (3) the definitions of the four needs are our summary of the paper's Section 1; (4) the background questions (role, gender, familiarity with Tenjin) are additions.",
 "author": "Huang, Yu, Lyu, Sun, Dewancker & Gao",
